Current classification concerns operating-cycle or near-term realisation and settlement; other items are non-current under applicable rules.|A loan due soon may be current unless the required deferral rights exist at the reporting date.|Classification mainly helps assess…?|Timing and liquidity|Market share|Product quality
Cash is money available; cash equivalents are short-term highly liquid investments meeting specified criteria and insignificant value-change risk.|Opening cash ₹15 plus net inflow ₹5 gives closing cash ₹20.|Is every traded investment a cash equivalent?|No|Yes|Only if profitable
Restricted cash has limits on use; liquid investments may be sellable but carry price or access risk.|Cash ₹20 includes ₹8 pledged for a loan: only ₹12 is freely available under the assumed restriction.|Freely available cash is…?|₹12|₹20|₹28
Trade receivables are customer balances; expected credit-loss allowances reduce their carrying value for anticipated credit shortfalls.|Gross receivables ₹30 less allowance ₹2 gives net receivables ₹28.|Raising the allowance collects customer cash?|No|Yes|Only after default
Inventory moves from raw materials through work in progress to finished goods. Cost is compared with net realisable value.|Raw materials ₹10, WIP ₹15, finished goods ₹25 total ₹50.|Selling finished goods converts their carrying cost into…?|Cost of sales|New share capital|A borrowing
Prepayments pay for future services; advances and other current assets require analysis of recoverability and settlement.|₹12 annual insurance paid upfront leaves ₹11 prepaid after one month.|The remaining ₹11 is automatically current-period expense?|No|Yes|Only if paid by bank
PPE represents qualifying tangible assets used over multiple periods. Carrying value reflects additions, depreciation, disposals, and impairment.|₹100 opening PPE + ₹20 additions − ₹5 depreciation = ₹115, ignoring other changes.|Closing PPE is…?|₹115|₹125|₹95
Capital work in progress records qualifying construction costs before an asset is ready for use.|₹30 factory construction is transferred to operating PPE when available for use, subject to applicable rules.|Transfer from CWIP to PPE necessarily creates new cash flow?|No|Yes|Only for buildings
Right-of-use assets represent a lessee's right to use leased property. They generally coexist with lease liabilities under IFRS 16, subject to exemptions.|A qualifying lease initially records a use-right asset and payment obligation rather than ownership of the property.|The right-of-use asset necessarily means legal ownership?|No|Yes|Only for offices
Intangibles lack physical substance; goodwill arises from qualifying acquisitions. Identifiability, useful life, and impairment rules differ.|Purchase price ₹120 less identifiable net assets ₹100 gives simplified goodwill ₹20.|Internally generated reputation automatically becomes goodwill on the balance sheet?|No|Yes|Only if famous
Financial investments are claims on financial assets; investment property is held for rent or capital appreciation under relevant definitions.|A rented building differs from an owner-occupied factory and from shares in a property company.|Which directly represents investment property?|A qualifying rented building|A share certificate|An operating machine
Deferred tax reflects certain temporary differences between accounting carrying amounts and tax bases, subject to recognition rules.|Tax depreciation exceeding book depreciation can create a deferred tax liability.|A deferred tax liability necessarily means a bill payable tomorrow?|No|Yes|Only if current tax is zero
Payables reflect received supplier goods or services; accrued expenses record incurred unpaid costs without necessarily an invoice.|₹10 supplier purchases and ₹4 payment leave ₹6 payable.|Paying that ₹6 recognises the original purchase again?|No|Yes|Only when overdue
Contract assets are conditional rights to consideration; receivables are unconditional apart from time; contract liabilities reflect obligations after advance consideration.|A customer pays ₹10 before delivery: cash and contract liability rise.|The advance necessarily increases current revenue?|No|Yes|Only for services
Borrowings are financing obligations split by applicable maturity and classification rules. Track principal, interest, security, and covenants.|A new ₹10 loan increases cash and debt by ₹10.|This loan creates operating revenue?|No|Yes|Only if spent on inventory
Lease liabilities reflect obligations for qualifying lease payments. Interest accrues and principal falls as payments are made.|Opening liability ₹100 + ₹10 interest − ₹30 payment = ₹80.|The entire ₹30 payment is interest expense?|No|Yes|Only for operating premises
Provisions cover qualifying uncertain obligations; pension and employee benefits require benefit-specific measurement and recognition.|A reliable qualifying obligation estimate of ₹5 can be recognised before cash settlement.|Management may create provisions purely to smooth earnings?|No|Yes|Only in profitable years
Share capital and securities premium reflect owner funding; retained earnings accumulate recognised earnings less distributions and adjustments.|Issue shares for ₹100 with ₹10 nominal capital: simplified capital ₹10 and premium ₹90.|Retained earnings are a separate cash account?|No|Yes|Only after an audit
Reserves capture specified equity components; treasury shares reduce equity; NCI represents outside ownership in controlled subsidiaries.|Repurchase own shares for ₹10: cash and group equity fall in the simplified entry.|Treasury shares are ordinarily an asset of the issuer?|No|Yes|Only if resale is planned
Contingencies and commitments may require disclosure rather than recognition, depending on obligation probability and measurement rules.|A guarantee may create future payment exposure even without a current funded loan.|Can disclosed guarantees affect an analyst's risk assessment?|Yes|No|Only when already paid
